<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Chambord</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Chambord was an alliance concluded in January 1552 between King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> and German Protestant princes led by Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. In return for French money, the princes allowed Henry to occupy the imperial cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> held from then on.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-chambord.jpg" alt="Treaty of Chambord" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Calips · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_Loir-et-Cher_Chambord_Chateau_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the Schmalkaldic War Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> defeated a number of rebellious Protestant princes, and in 1548 he issued the sharply worded Augsburg Interim to bring the Lutheran movement back into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and prevent a split of the Empire. To defend their autonomy, several Protestant Imperial States formed the Alliance of Torgau in 1551. Chief among them was Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, who had strongly supported the emperor in the Schmalkaldic War, together with William IV of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, Margrave Albert Alcibiades of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert, Duke of Prussia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 15 January 1552 at the Château de Chambord. Maurice ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the vicariate over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and Henry II promised military and economic aid against the emperor. The German princes had neither the right nor any authority to give away imperial territory, so by legal standards the accord had no force. Its text described the bishoprics as not of German language, implying that the transfer was limited to non-German lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Maurice, then charged by the emperor with subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, made peace with the city and changed sides, while French troops marched to occupy the bishoprics. The princes' forces campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> to flee towards Villach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, and in August 1552 his brother Archduke Ferdinand I of Austria concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a>, accepting the Lutheran Augsburg Confession, which the emperor confirmed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555.</p><p>During the Italian War of 1551–59 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> tried to regain the bishoprics by force, but his troops were defeated by Duke Francis of Guise at the Battle of Renty in 1554. Ferdinand I made no further attempt, and the Empire acknowledged their incorporation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648. The treaty set a pattern for French policy in the following decades, which used conflicts between the emperor and the German princes to France's own advantage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Chambord” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1552 Henry II of France occupied Metz under the <b>Treaty of Chambord</b> with German Protestant princes.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-metz">Free Imperial City of Metz</a></li></ul></section>
